            <ArticleTitle>Off-pump coronary artery bypass surgery--initial experience in Gdansk: a brief review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>240-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Before the introduction of cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB), coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) procedures were performed on a beating heart. In 1967, Kolessov first reported &quot;off-pump&quot; revascularization of the left anterior descending artery (LAD). This technique was later abandoned when the use of cardioplegia and the heart-lung machine allowed a motionless and bloodless operative field. This study reports our initial clinical experience in off-pump coronary bypass surgery performed at the Department of Cardiac Surgery of the Medical University of Gdansk, Poland.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study enrolled all consecutive patients who were operated on at our institution without CPB between January 1998 and December 2001. Patients were selected for the off-pump procedure individually by the surgeon. Demographics, operative procedures, postoperative mortality, morbidity, and early outcomes were analyzed. The observation period included 30 postoperative days.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">An average of 1.8 grafts per patient were completed. Conversion to CPB was required in 4.1% of patients. Complete revascularization was attained in 91% of the procedures. Surgical mortality was 1.3% and perioperative myocardial infarction was reported in 2.4%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In selected patients, off-pump CABG may be used as a suitable and safe alternative to conventional on-pump coronary surgery and permits complete revascularization with comparable short-term results.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
